<title>MEXICO: Mexico '97 copra output seen down at 170,000 T.</title>
<headline>Mexico '97 copra output seen down at 170,000 T.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-03-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico's 1997 copra production is seen down slightly at 170,000 tonnes this year, the head of the National Association of Copra Producers told state-owned news agency Notimex.</p>
<p>Jose Antonio Tejeda Garcia said the crop outlook was slightly lower than the previous two years because of damage from hurricanes that passed through coconut groves in 1995 and 1996.</p>
<p>Copra, the dried kernel of coconuts, is cultivated mainly in three Mexican states -- Tabasco, Guerrero and Colima. Mexico has around 200,000 hectares under coconut tree production, Tejeda told Notimex.</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7287902))</p>
